  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Suppose $x$ and $y$ are positive real numbers. Prove that\n$$\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}>\\sqrt{x+y}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The contrary inequality gives $2\\sqrt{xy}\\le0$, contradicting $x,y>0$.",
      "worked": "Suppose the contrary. Both sides are positive, so squaring preserves the inequality.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}&\\le\\sqrt{x+y}\\\\(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^2&\\le(\\sqrt{x+y})^2\\\\x+2\\sqrt{xy}+y&\\le x+y\\\\2\\sqrt{xy}&\\le0.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $x,y>0$, we have $2\\sqrt{xy}>0$, a contradiction. Therefore, $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}>\\sqrt{x+y}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
